.TH KOSH 5 "2026-10-25" "kosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kosh.conf \- Koshka settings files and the KOSHCONF encoding
.SH DESCRIPTION
Koshka settings files hold option values, not shell commands. Every shell loads
them before any startup file, interactive or not, and the configured mood also
applies to
.B kosh \-c
and to a script without a shebang. The
.B koshconf
builtin of
.BR kosh (1)
loads, changes, and writes them, and the OPTION REGISTRY section there lists
the options.
.PP
No settings file or KOSHCONF is applied by a shell started with
.BR \-Q ,
.BR \-\-no\-config ,
or
.BR \-p ;
a lint, format, or language server run; rescue mode; a shell whose real and
effective ids differ; a subshell, which receives its state from its parent; or
a binary started as a koshkit utility or as
.BR koshkit .
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf " or " %ProgramData%\ekosh\ekosh.conf
The system settings file, loaded first and only when no other user could have
written it. On POSIX, the file and its directory must be owned by root and not
writable by group or others. On Windows, the file and its
.I kosh
directory must be owned by SYSTEM, Administrators, or TrustedInstaller, and no
access control entry may let another account modify them, change their
permissions, or take ownership; the directory may let others add files. A file
that fails the check is skipped with a warning.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf " or " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file, loaded second. XDG_CONFIG_HOME applies when it is an
absolute path. On POSIX, a shell whose effective user is root neither loads nor
writes it.
.BR "koshconf create" ,
.BR "koshconf set \-\-persist" ,
and
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
write it: a symbolic link is kept and its target written, the permission bits
of the replaced file are kept, a new file gets mode 0644 less the creation
mask, a missing directory is created, and the previous file is copied to
.I kosh.conf.bak
beside it.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh " or " ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh
The user rc script of a login or interactive
.B kosh
mood, described under RC SCRIPT.
.PP
The Bash and POSIX startup files that the other moods source are listed in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines ended by LF or CRLF, and a leading UTF-8
byte order mark is skipped. A line that is blank or starts with a number sign
after optional blanks is ignored. Every other line has the form
.IR name = value ,
with blanks around the name and value ignored. A value starting with a double
or single quote ends at the next quote of that kind, and only blanks may follow
it; quote a value whose surrounding blanks must be kept. A value cannot span
lines.
.PP
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0. An enumeration
is written as its value. A string must be valid UTF-8 without a NUL byte.
editor.history.max_entries is an integer from 0 to 2147483647, and
editor.wrap_width one from 0 to 65535. init_moods is a comma-separated list of
moods without empty elements. A name may be the canonical name or its legacy
alias.
.PP
A file may set every option except those fixed by how the shell started, such
as bash.interpreter.login_shell, and those that follow the session, such as
posix.interpreter.job_control.
.B A semantic option in a file changes every shell that loads it,
.B scripts included,
so set such an option for a session with
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
or
.B koshconf set
unless every script should use it.
.PP
The mood applies first, wherever it appears, and the other lines are read
under it. In the
.B kosh
mood, posix.interpreter.unset_variable_is_error,
posix.interpreter.pipeline_fails_on_any_stage,
bash.interpreter.glob_no_match_is_error, and
interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers stay on and
bash.interpreter.glob_no_match_expands_to_nothing stays off, and a line setting
another value is skipped with a warning. A later line replaces an earlier one,
and the user file and KOSHCONF replace the system file. An option given on the
command line, such as
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR "\-o pipefail" ,
or
.BR \-L ,
keeps its value, so
.B kosh +e script
runs without errexit whatever a file says, and a KOSH_HISTORY_FILE or
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE inherited from the environment overrides
editor.history.file_path or editor.history.max_entries.
.PP
.B koshconf
quotes a written value that starts with a quote or starts or ends with a blank,
and keeps the CRLF endings of a file that uses them. A value holding a newline,
or one that needs quoting and holds both quote characters, cannot be written,
and
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
rejects it before changing the session. Persisting mood=kosh also removes the
lines of the options the
.B kosh
mood fixes. A line whose option this version does not recognize is kept at the
end of a rewritten file.
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes every option a file may hold, each after a
.RI # " help"
comment and commented out with the preset value except the mood, so the system
file applies until a line is uncommented. A string option without a default is
an empty commented-out line. The
.B kosh
preset holds the values the
.B kosh
mood fixes, so uncommenting a line never warns.
.SH STARTUP MOODS
init_moods lists the moods whose startup files an interactive or login shell
sources, in order, each parsed under the grammar of its mood. It is empty by
default, which sources the files of the session mood, and so only the user rc
script in the
.B kosh
mood. It is the configured form of
.BR \-L ,
which replaces it. A shell that is neither interactive nor a login shell
ignores it, a restricted shell ignores it with a warning, and KOSHCONF never
carries it. To keep the
.B kosh
mood and still source ~/.bashrc, write
.PP
.RS
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.RE
.PP
in the user file. Every mood sets KOSH without exporting it, so a line in
~/.bashrc guarded by
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka.
.SH KOSHCONF
.B KOSHCONF
is a dynamic shell variable holding the mood and the interactive options in a
binary form encoded as standard base64 with padding. It is not exported unless
the user exports it; after
.BR "export KOSHCONF" ,
each started command receives the settings current at its start. A starting
shell applies a KOSHCONF from its environment after the settings files and
removes it before any command runs, and
.B koshconf load
applies a value to a running shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF="$KOSHCONF" kosh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records, each an option id, a value
length, and the value. The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints: seven
bits per byte, least significant group first, with the high bit set on every
byte but the last. A boolean is one byte, 0 or 1; an enumeration is a varint
holding the position of the value in its list; a string is its UTF-8 bytes. The
ids are listed in
.BR kosh (1)
and never change meaning. Semantic options never pass through the
environment; a child shell loads the settings files itself.
.PP
A record with an unknown id, an option the encoding never carries, or a value a
settings file would reject is skipped with a warning. Only the canonical form
is accepted: the base64 text carries its padding, every varint uses the fewest
bytes, and the ids strictly ascend. A value that breaks these rules, or has a
truncated record, is rejected as a whole with a warning.
.SH ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped. An unreadable file, a file over 1 MiB, one
that is not a regular file, and a system file that fails the ownership check
produce a warning and are skipped. A line without an equals sign, an unknown
option, an invalid value, an unclosed quote, text after a closing quote, and an
option a file cannot hold each produce a warning with the file and line, and
the line is skipped. A settings file never stops the shell.
.SH RC SCRIPT
A login or interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood sources scripts/rc.kosh in the user configuration directory after the
settings files; the script can test
.B shopt \-q login_shell
for login commands. No system rc script, ~/.koshrc, or /etc/koshrc is read. In
an interactive non-login shell,
.B \-\-rcfile
replaces the script, and
.B \-\-norc
or privileged mode skips it. Shell code belongs in the script, and options in
.IR kosh.conf .
.PP
A file that the
.B kosh
mood sources with
.B .
runs in the
.B bash
mood with its lax options when it is a Bash startup file (.bashrc,
\&.bash_profile, .bash_login, .bash_logout, .profile, or /etc/profile), and
otherwise in the mood its shebang selects. The
.B kosh
mood returns when the file ends, while the variables, functions, and aliases it
defines stay. Analysis does not follow such a file, a syntax error in it fails
the
.B .
command without ending the shell, and the files it sources keep its mood.
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes this script when it is missing and never replaces it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
if shopt \-q login_shell; then
  [ \-r /etc/profile ] && . /etc/profile
  if [ \-r ~/.bash_profile ]; then
    . ~/.bash_profile
  elif [ \-r ~/.bash_login ]; then
    . ~/.bash_login
  elif [ \-r ~/.profile ]; then
    . ~/.profile
  fi
else
  [ \-r ~/.profile ] && . ~/.profile
  [ \-r ~/.bashrc ] && . ~/.bashrc
fi
.EE
.RE
.PP
A KOSH_WELCOME or KOSH_FAREWELL value of zero display width disables that
message:
.PP
.RS
.EX
printf \-v KOSH_WELCOME \(dq\er\(dq
printf \-v KOSH_FAREWELL \(dq\er\(dq
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
A user file that keeps the Bash grammar and edits with vi keys:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mood=bash
editor.history.max_entries=20000
editor.base_mode=vi
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B koshconf set
.I option value
.B \-\-persist
writes the same lines.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (1)
